<h2>Europe's front door for Indian produce</h2>
<p>The Netherlands is less a single national market and more the entry point for a large share of Indian agricultural trade into the whole EU. Rotterdam's scale, cold-chain infrastructure and distribution connections make it the practical choice even for goods ultimately headed to Germany, France or beyond.</p>

<p>Grapes are the clearest example — Netherlands consistently ranks as a top destination in Indian grape trade data precisely because of this redistribution role.</p>

<h2>EU compliance is the whole conversation</h2>
<p>Once goods are headed for the Netherlands, you're dealing with EU food law, not a national standard. Two things matter most:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>TRACES registration.</strong> Phytosanitary and health certificates for plant and animal products are registered through the EU's TRACES platform. This is normally handled on the importer's side, but the certificate has to be right on our side first.</li>
  <li><strong>Residue limits.</strong> EU MRLs are strict and enforced at the border. This is the single most common cause of rejection for Indian fresh produce and spices entering the EU — pre-shipment testing is cheap insurance against an expensive problem.</li>
</ul>
